<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=pyramid-of-djoser">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Building and engineering</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pyramid of Djoser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">27th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/27th-century-bce">27th century BCE</a> · Also known as Step Pyramid of Djoser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pyramid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a>, or Step Pyramid, is a six-stepped stone pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, built for the Third Dynasty king Djoser in the 27th century BCE. Attributed to his official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a>, it began as a mastaba tomb and was enlarged in stages, and it is regarded as the earliest large cut-stone building. It stands within a walled complex of courts and ceremonial buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pyramid-of-djoser.jpg" alt="Pyramid of Djoser" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olaf Tausch · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sakkara_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Pyramid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a> stands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> necropolis northwest of the ruins of Memphis, and it was the first Egyptian pyramid to be built. Djoser, known in his tomb by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name Netjerikhet, was the first or second king of the 3rd Dynasty. Most Egyptologists credit his vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a> with the design, on the strength of a statue of Imhotep in the complex, his title of overseer of sculptors and painters, and a remark by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> that he invented building in stone. An earlier mastaba at Saqqara, S3038, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> walls built out into eight shallow steps, was a precedent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The pyramid rises in six steps to between 60 and 62.5 m, with a base of 109 m by 121 m, and was cased in polished white limestone. It was built in phases, labelled M1 to M3 and P1 to P2 after the excavator Jean-Philippe Lauer. It began as a square mastaba 63 m wide, was enlarged with new casings and an eastern extension over eleven shafts leading to the burial chambers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djoser">Djoser</a>'s family, then became a four-step pyramid and finally a six-step one. Egyptologists disagree on why: Lauer thought the change made the tomb visible from Memphis, while Rainer Stadelmann suggested it was planned as a pyramid from the start.</p><p>Beneath it lies a labyrinth of tunnels and galleries nearly 6 km long, running from a central shaft 28 m deep. Some walls are inlaid with blue faience tile in imitation of reed matting. The burial chamber was a vault of granite closed with a block of 3.5 tons; no body was found, since the tomb had been extensively robbed. More than 40,000 stone vessels were found in the galleries, many bearing the names of earlier kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The pyramid stands in a mortuary complex of about 15 hectares, enclosed by a niched limestone wall 10.5 m high and over 1.6 km long and surrounded by a rock-cut trench 750 m long. Its builders carved 1,680 niches in the wall by hand, copying forms that earlier builders had made from wood, rope and reed mats. The stone work implies that the royal government now controlled far more material and human resources, and from this reign the kings of the Old Kingdom were buried in the north rather than at Abydos.</p><p>The step pyramid was the forerunner of the pyramids of the 4th, 5th and 6th Dynasties, including those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giza">Giza</a>. Imhotep was later deified and known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as Asclepios. The monument was reopened to visitors in March 2020 after a restoration of 14 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pyramid of Djoser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2023 Egypt's Ministry of Antiquities announced sections on a 16-metre papyrus found near the <b>Step Pyramid of Djoser</b>, now called the Waziri Papyrus I.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>27th century BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Saqqara, Egypt</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/imhotep">Imhotep</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Some Egyptologists think the nearby enclosure wall Gisr el-Mudir is older; the credit to Imhotep is that of modern Egyptologists.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">Mudbrick<span>c. 9000 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/copper-smelting">Copper smelting<span>c. 5000 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=pyramid-of-djoser">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
